welcome back to las vegas now in around  healthcare the sponsors of this next  segment wants to remind adults  especially our elderly that vaccines are  not just for kids so we spoke to a  doctor to learn more  [Music]  hi my name is denita calra i'm a family  physician for intermountain healthcare  today we're at our green valley a my  generation clinic and what we do here is  we help take care of our adults and  senior population today we're going to  talk about why vaccines are important  not only for our children but for adults  in general there are some conditions  that we need to re-vaccinate for or  vaccinate originally for because as we  get older our conditions and our  diseases change and everything is  sounding very good today there are four  suggested vaccines adults should stay up  to date on  pneumococcal hepatitis b  zoster and influenza let's start with  the pneumococcal vaccine a new  pneumococcal vaccine has been released  for people aged 65 and older who haven't  been vaccinated or under 65 and immune  compromised or with a chronic condition  that puts them at risk of pneumonia such  as asthma and smoking  the pneumococcal vaccine can prevent  some cases of pneumonia meningitis and  sepsis i strongly recommend pneumococcal  vaccine especially in our environment  because of the strong changes in our  temperature including dust winds and all  the allergens in the air now let's move  on to hepatitis b hepatitis b vaccine is  recommended for all adults just like it  is for all kids  this vaccine protects against liver  cancer and liver disease two-thirds of  those who get hepatitis b don't have a  known risk factor for it but it can  develop a serous infection if not  protected the zoster shingles vaccine is  recommended for people 50 years and  older  shingles is a viral infection that  causes a painful rash  anyone who has had chickenpox may  develop shingles if you have certain  health conditions that make you immune  compromised it may be recommended  starting at the age of 19. and of course  the influenza vaccine the seasonal  influenza vaccine is recommended to  everyone age six months and older and it  generally starts early fall some people  feel that if they got the influenza  vaccine once they don't have to get it  again but it is very important to get it  every year for the virus can change and  besides those four very important  vaccines we still need to keep covet in  mind new versions of this vaccine are  coming soon a recombinant protein  vaccine by nova vaxx may be available  for those who haven't been vaccinated  yet and for those who have been  vaccinated boosters targeting more  recent variants are being studied and  just a reminder many vaccines are  covered by your health insurance here at  intermountain healthcare we're happy to  set up a preventative visit to help you  decide which vaccines are good for you  [Music]  thank you to intermountain healthcare  for sponsoring that segment for more  just head to intermountainhealthcare.org  nevada
 

